WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:08.347
Parents have known for thousands of years that physical characteristics—such as curly hair or brown eyes—seem to run in the family.

2
00:00:08.348 --> 00:00:16.903
These characteristics are called inherited traits, and the transmission of traits from generation to generation is called heredity.

3
00:00:16.904 --> 00:00:27.121
Farmers realized the power of inherited traits very early on—thousands of years ago, when plants were first being cultivated and animals were first being domesticated.

4
00:00:27.122 --> 00:00:40.881
When selecting individuals to breed, a farmer would choose the stock with the best traits—the strongest oxen, the fastest horses, or the plants with the largest fruit—in the hope that their offspring would have the same characteristics.

5
00:00:40.882 --> 00:00:47.270
By the nineteenth century, it still wasn't known exactly how those traits were passed from one generation to another.

6
00:00:47.271 --> 00:00:52.874
Gregor Mendel, now known as the father of genetics, studied the hereditary patterns of pea plants.

7
00:00:52.875 --> 00:01:01.179
Starting in 1856, he spent years performing experiments and observing traits like flower color, seed shape, and stem length.

8
00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:08.735
For example, Mendel bred a pea plant that produced only green peas with another that produced only yellow peas.

9
00:01:08.736 --> 00:01:14.861
By doing this, he created a hybrid generation of pea plants, one that produced only yellow peas.

10
00:01:14.862 --> 00:01:18.671
Then he took some of those hybrid plants and bred them with each other.

11
00:01:18.672 --> 00:01:24.281
The green trait reappeared in approximately a quarter of the peas in the next generation.

12
00:01:24.282 --> 00:01:31.712
This showed Mendel that individuals can pass a characteristic to the next generation even if they do not express that trait.

13
00:01:31.713 --> 00:01:36.387
These observations led Mendel to conclude that every trait is controlled by "factors."

14
00:01:36.388 --> 00:01:42.058
Each individual receives two of these factors for each trait—one version from each parent.

15
00:01:42.059 --> 00:01:47.811
Sometimes, one version is expressed, and at other times, the other version is expressed.

16
00:01:47.812 --> 00:01:53.891
Meanwhile, British scientist Charles Darwin had also been observing traits and experimenting.

17
00:01:53.892 --> 00:02:02.807
Darwin was puzzled by physical variations that had been absent in previous generations but suddenly appeared in a new generation of an organism.

18
00:02:02.808 --> 00:02:10.253
He proposed that the characteristics of a species change over time through a process he called "natural selection."

19
00:02:10.254 --> 00:02:16.799
Individuals that are not well adapted to their environment die young and do not have a chance to reproduce.

20
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:23.520
Other individuals are better adapted, with features that make them more competitive in the struggle for survival and mates.

21
00:02:23.521 --> 00:02:26.346
These features are known as adaptations.

22
00:02:26.347 --> 00:02:32.966
Darwin observed examples of adaptation in a group of birds known now as Darwin's finches.

23
00:02:32.967 --> 00:02:39.683
Originally all one species, the finches had spread to different islands within the Galapagos Archipelago.

24
00:02:39.684 --> 00:02:46.429
Over time, the beaks of these finches changed, becoming suited to whatever food was available in each area.

25
00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:51.323
As a result, the one species had branched out into 14 species.

26
00:02:51.324 --> 00:03:00.658
Darwin’s great insight—that over many generations, species can change dramatically to better fit their environments—is known as the theory of evolution.

27
00:03:00.659 --> 00:03:07.586
He published his ideas in 1859 in a groundbreaking book called On the Origin of Species

28
00:03:07.587 --> 00:03:12.098
. But Darwin still did not understand the biology behind this

29
00:03:12.099 --> 00:03:21.355
process. The modern science of heredity is known as genetics. In the decades following the work of Mendel and Darwin, scientists continued to study

30
00:03:21.356 --> 00:03:30.522
heredity. By the 1940s, scientists had deduced that at the center of the puzzle is a molecule that they called deoxyribonucleic acid

31
00:03:30.523 --> 00:03:35.570
(DNA). DNA contains the biological set of instructions that make each species

32
00:03:35.571 --> 00:03:41.681
unique. What makes an elephant an elephant? DNA. What makes a tulip a tulip?

33
00:03:41.682 --> 00:03:53.390
DNA. Minor changes in DNA, called mutations, lead to changes in the structure and behavior of organisms and, over millions and millions of years, lead to new and different life

34
00:03:53.391 --> 00:03:57.003
forms. This is the process behind Darwin's theory of evolution.

35
00:03:57.004 --> 00:04:03.924
Although researchers were relatively sure that DNA was the key to inherited traits, they didn't know what it looked like.

36
00:04:03.925 --> 00:04:09.261
In 1953, the scientific community was racing to discover its structure.

37
00:04:09.262 --> 00:04:14.087
One team, at Cambridge University, was led by James Watson and Francis Crick.

38
00:04:14.088 --> 00:04:21.242
Researchers Maurice Wilkins and Rosalind Franklin, at King's College in London, were also working on the problem.

39
00:04:21.243 --> 00:04:29.036
Franklin used x-rays to make images of a DNA sample, and she suspected that the structure was a helix shape.

40
00:04:29.037 --> 00:04:35.337
Her partner showed Franklin's research to Watson and Crick. The Cambridge team solved the rest of the puzzle.

41
00:04:35.338 --> 00:04:41.629
DNA was helix-shaped, but it was a double helix—a double-stranded, twisted molecule.

42
00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:51.946
This discovery won Watson, Crick, and Wilkins the 1962 Nobel Prize for Physiology or Medicine. (Franklin had died of illness in 1958.)

43
00:04:51.947 --> 00:04:57.336
The work of these two teams allowed other scientists to unravel the mystery of DNA.

44
00:04:57.337 --> 00:05:05.885
DNA is found in the nucleus of every cell. It is organized into structures called chromosomes. Chromosomes are made up of genes.

45
00:05:05.886 --> 00:05:14.935
Human chromosomes have tens of thousands of genes, and all of these genes together—known as a genome—control an organism's characteristics.

46
00:05:14.936 --> 00:05:20.101
Further discoveries in the twentieth century paved the way for amazing advances in genetics.

47
00:05:20.102 --> 00:05:24.133
In the 1970s, "genetic engineering" became possible.

48
00:05:24.134 --> 00:05:29.760
Scientists learned how to take a gene from one organism and combine it with the DNA of another.

49
00:05:29.761 --> 00:05:38.681
For example, a gene that keeps flounder from freezing in cold water was cut from the chromosome of the fish and inserted into a tomato's DNA.

50
00:05:38.682 --> 00:05:42.339
This change resulted in a tomato less vulnerable to frost.

51
00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:48.637
Genes have been inserted into many other crops to make the plants resistant to disease or to increase production.

52
00:05:48.638 --> 00:05:59.955
Although genetically modified crops are becoming quite common, many believe that they pose a significant danger to the environment and human health because their long-term effects are not known.

53
00:05:59.956 --> 00:06:05.272
Promising—and somewhat less controversial—advances have also been made in the area of human genetics.

54
00:06:05.273 --> 00:06:11.653
In the 1980s, scientists developed methods to compare the DNA sequence of different individuals.

55
00:06:11.654 --> 00:06:21.041
DNA left behind at the scene of a crime has been used as evidence in court, both to prosecute criminals and to set free people who have been wrongly accused.

56
00:06:21.042 --> 00:06:24.930
DNA sequencing techniques are also useful in the field of medicine.

57
00:06:24.931 --> 00:06:32.420
Studying families with rare genetic disorders has allowed doctors to trace the genetic basis of disease through generations.

58
00:06:32.421 --> 00:06:41.312
This kind of genetic tracking helps doctors to predict the likelihood of a person getting a disease and to diagnose it—although not to cure the illness.

59
00:06:41.313 --> 00:06:49.755
However, some genetic diseases can now be treated by replacing damaged genes with healthy ones, a practice called gene therapy.

60
00:06:49.756 --> 00:07:04.129
In many cases, it's not possible or ethical to experiment on humans, so scientists often rely on other organisms, ones that reproduce rapidly such as mice or fruit flies, to learn more about the effects of gene therapy.

61
00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:09.058
The twenty-first century continues to be a time of technological advancement in genetics.

62
00:07:09.059 --> 00:07:15.220
The first milestone of the twenty-first century was the completion of the Human Genome Project in 2003.

63
00:07:15.221 --> 00:07:21.657
Researchers now know the DNA sequence of all human genes, and are making a detailed map of each chromosome.

64
00:07:21.658 --> 00:07:28.370
Doctors are able to take a sample of a person's DNA, scan it, and determine his or her genetic risk.

65
00:07:28.371 --> 00:07:34.258
New mysteries and concerns will undoubtedly arise as scientists continue to explore genetics.

66
00:07:34.259 --> 00:07:41.380
But their discoveries will also answer many questions about the biology of the world's species and the code of life.

